then(met) :- _(met@1)

// The existence views. A row answers YES to the proposition; absence
// answers NO — there is no `met = 0` row, because a false receipt that
// still occupies a row would survive every conjunction it joins.
// The witness produces a row either way, so the filter is what turns
// its 0 into an absence.
exists(T(*))(*) :- T(+), met = 1
notexists(T(*))(*) :- T(\+), met = 1

// `equals` is deliberately NOT here. A view built on the relational
// minus pairs columns by name; the comparison `equals` lowers to pairs
// them by position, and the two disagree wherever a heading repeats or
// reorders names. It lands as a native binary predicate instead.
